<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tonle Sap</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tonle Sap is a large freshwater lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> connected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong River</a>, which swells greatly during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> season. Its seasonal flooding and rich fisheries supported dense populations and intensive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> agriculture. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> treat the Tonle Sap region as part of the environmental foundation of Khmer power around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tonle-sap.jpg" alt="Tonle Sap" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Deror_avi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cambodia,_Tonle_Sap_IMG_3285.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
